     SOURCE_HASH=sha512:946c709564fa0b97e38736bd6f8d236f251b586f787559f65dd1f8f9c9fc2b123823ab623c1979cffb7a184698ffd5dd49fd4b37e95c33cac3d78e07f78c03b8
    SOURCE2_HASH=sha512:4476ffedbd44e28f19528fe8858c8de7c96c8b93ce1a76abed9a560d0ca9e36e07b821939908246373cbe78f2b7ab8cdca651660a7578e6f823a13f9c5590b96
SOURCE_DIRECTORY="${BUILD_DIRECTORY}/broadcom-sta-${VERSION}"
        WEB_SITE="https://www.broadcom.com/support/download-search?pg=Legacy+Products&pf=Legacy+Wireless&pn=&pa=&po=&dk=&pl="
        KEYWORDS="kernel wireless driver broadcom"
      LICENSE[0]="Broadcom proprietary (hybrid: source glue + binary blob)"
         ENTERED=20261106
           SHORT="Broadcom BCM43xx 802.11 wireless driver (proprietary wl, hybrid)"
cat << EOF
Broadcom's proprietary "hybrid" Linux driver (wl.ko) for BCM4311, BCM4312,
BCM4321, BCM4322, BCM43142, BCM43224, BCM43225, BCM43227, BCM43228, BCM4331,
BCM4360 and BCM43602 PCI/PCIe wireless chips, e.g. the BCM4360 in 2013-2014
MacBook Airs, which the free b43 and brcmsmac drivers do not support.

The kernel-facing glue is shipped as source and compiled against your
running kernel; the rest of the driver is a binary blob (wlc_hybrid.o).
Broadcom's own download is gone and the driver does not build on modern
kernels as released, so this spell uses Debian's 6.30.223.271 packaging,
whose patch series (linux 3.x through 7.x) is applied before building.

The in-kernel b43, b43legacy, bcma, ssb and brcmsmac drivers conflict with
wl; the spell installs /etc/modprobe.d/broadcom-wl.conf to blacklist them.
Your kernel needs CONFIG_CFG80211 (as a module or built in) and PCI.

Rebuilt automatically whenever the linux spell is cast.
EOF
